  "contracts": {
    "lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-qed-formal-ring",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the finite graph phase data of [[def-qft-qed-finite-graph-gauge-data]] and the planned QED $U(1)$, Clifford, and natural-unit conventions of [[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]]. Let $K_0(\\zeta)\\in GL_d(\\mathbb C)$ be an invertible base matrix and let $V(\\zeta)\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ be its finite link-hopping matrix. The admissible covariant families satisfy\n$$\nK_0(\\zeta^\\alpha)=D_\\alpha K_0(\\zeta)D_\\alpha^{-1},\\qquad\nV(\\zeta^\\alpha)=D_\\alpha V(\\zeta)D_\\alpha^{-1}.\n$$\nThe scalar base $K_0=mI_d$, $m\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$, is an explicit admissible case. Define\n$$\nK(z;\\zeta):=K_0(\\zeta)+zV(\\zeta),\\qquad\nS(z;\\zeta):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(-zK_0(\\zeta)^{-1}V(\\zeta))^nK_0(\\zeta)^{-1}.\n$$\nThe coefficient of each power $z^n$ in $S$ is one finite matrix. The item defines this formal candidate; the following lemma proves its two-sided inverse and covariance properties. Define $\\det A$ for a finite matrix $A$ over $\\mathbb C\\llbracket z\\rrbracket$ by the Leibniz formula $\\det A=\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_d}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)\\prod_{j=1}^d A_{j,\\sigma(j)}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qft-qed-finite-graph-gauge-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a real vertex phase $\\theta=(\\theta_x)_{x\\in X}\\in\\mathbb R^X$, define\n$$\n\\zeta_e^\\theta:=e^{i(\\theta_{s(e)}-\\theta_{t(e)})}\\zeta_e,\\qquad\nD_\\theta:=\\operatorname{diag}_{x\\in X}(e^{i\\theta_x}I_r)\\in GL_d(\\mathbb C).\n$$\nDefine the link-hopping matrix $V(\\zeta)\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ by its $x,y$ block indexed by $x,y\\in X$\n$$\nV(\\zeta)_{xy}:=\\sum_{\\substack{e\\in E\\\\s(e)=x,\\ t(e)=y}}T_e\\zeta_e .\n$$\nAn empty sum is the zero $r\\times r$ matrix. Additional fixed diagonal blocks belong to a separate base matrix. These data are finite-dimensional algebraic data; the phases are external parameters, not rotor multiplication operators on $L^2(\\mathbb T^E)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-qft-qed-gauge-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a real smooth gauge parameter $\\chi$ in webers, put $\\alpha=\\chi/S_0$ (dimensionless) and use\n$$A'_a=A_a+\\partial_a\\chi,\\qquad a'_a=a_a+\\partial_a\\alpha,\\qquad \\psi'=e^{ig\\alpha}\\psi.$$\nThe induced covariant derivative is $D'_a=\\partial_a-ig a'_a$. These are formal mathematical conventions for the later conditional deductions. They do not adopt a measured value of $\\mu_0$, identify $q$ with an electron charge, impose charge quantization, or assert that interacting continuum fields exist.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward-step-1-1",
          "claim": "Put $B:=K_0^{-1}V$. The coefficient of $z^n$ in $S$ is $(-B)^nK_0^{-1}$, a finite matrix, so the series defines an element of $M_d(\\mathbb C[[z]])$ without an analytic convergence assumption.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward-step-1-2",
          "claim": "Since $K=K_0(I+zB)$, multiplication gives $KS=K_0(I+zB)\\sum_{n\\ge0}(-zB)^nK_0^{-1}$. In the product $(I+zB)\\sum_{n\\ge0}(-zB)^n$, the constant coefficient is $I$ and the two contributions to every positive-degree coefficient cancel; hence $KS=I$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward-step-1-3",
          "claim": "Also $K_0^{-1}K=I+zB$, so $SK=\\sum_{n\\ge0}(-zB)^n(I+zB)=I$ by the same coefficientwise cancellation. Thus $S$ is the two-sided formal inverse.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward-step-2-1",
          "claim": "Each link block transforms as $T_e\\zeta_e^\\theta=e^{i\\theta_{s(e)}}(T_e\\zeta_e)e^{-i\\theta_{t(e)}}$; summing links in each block gives $V(\\zeta^\\theta)=D_\\theta V(\\zeta)D_\\theta^{-1}$. With [A1], the assumed covariance of $K_0$ gives the same conjugation for each coefficient of $K$; the QED phase interpretation is exactly $\\theta_x=g\\alpha_x$ from [F3].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward-step-2-2",
          "claim": "Substituting the conjugated $K_0,V$ into the defining series for $S$ conjugates each finite coefficient by $D_\\theta$, proving $S(z;\\zeta^\\theta)=D_\\theta S(z;\\zeta)D_\\theta^{-1}$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward-step-3-1",
          "claim": "Differentiate $K_sS_s=I$ coefficientwise at $s=0$. This gives $(\\delta K)S+K(\\delta S)=0$, hence $\\delta S=-S(\\delta K)S$ using $K^{-1}=S$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward-step-3-2",
          "claim": "From $D_s=e^{is\\Theta}$, direct differentiation gives $\\delta K=i[\\Theta,K]$. Therefore $-S\\,i[\\Theta,K]\\,S=-iS\\Theta KS+iSK\\Theta S=-iS\\Theta+i\\Theta S=i[\\Theta,S]$.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "For d=1 and V=0, S=K0^{-1}; steps 1.2–1.3 give both inverse products."
        },
