          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Use the finite graph phase data of [[def-qft-qed-finite-graph-gauge-data]] and the planned QED $U(1)$, Clifford, and natural-unit conventions of [[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]]. Let $K_0(\\zeta)\\in GL_d(\\mathbb C)$ be an invertible base matrix and let $V(\\zeta)\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ be its finite link-hopping matrix. The admissible covariant families satisfy\n$$\nK_0(\\zeta^\\alpha)=D_\\alpha K_0(\\zeta)D_\\alpha^{-1},\\qquad\nV(\\zeta^\\alpha)=D_\\alpha V(\\zeta)D_\\alpha^{-1}.\n$$\nThe scalar base $K_0=mI_d$, $m\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$, is an explicit admissible case. Define\n$$\nK(z;\\zeta):=K_0(\\zeta)+zV(\\zeta),\\qquad\nS(z;\\zeta):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(-zK_0(\\zeta)^{-1}V(\\zeta))^nK_0(\\zeta)^{-1}.\n$$\nThe coefficient of each power $z^n$ in $S$ is one finite matrix. The item defines this formal candidate; the following lemma proves its two-sided inverse and covariance properties. Define $\\det A$ for a finite matrix $A$ over $\\mathbb C\\llbracket z\\rrbracket$ by the Leibniz formula $\\det A=\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_d}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)\\prod_{j=1}^d A_{j,\\sigma(j)}$.",
