    "lem-qft-qed-tree-ward": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-qft-dirac-projections",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], the mathematical scalar shell conventions of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and the second-variable-linear Hilbert pairing of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. In the fixed inertial coordinates $x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] on the affine Minkowski model [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]], $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$; these coordinates have length units under [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. The parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ and momentum coordinate $\\mathbf k$ have inverse-length units, and $\\omega(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ has the same units. The parameter is not identified here with a physical rest mass. Matrix $h_D$ has inverse-length units; the dimensionless spin matrices have no physical unit assignment in this mathematical item.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-dirac-projections",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the Pauli matrices $\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}$, and $\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$. On $\\mathbb C^4=\\mathbb C^2\\oplus\\mathbb C^2$, define\n$$\\beta=\\begin{pmatrix}I_2&0\\\\0&-I_2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\alpha_j=\\begin{pmatrix}0&\\sigma_j\\\\\\sigma_j&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\gamma^0=\\beta,\\qquad \\gamma^j=\\beta\\alpha_j.$$\nFor a spinor column $z\\in\\mathbb C^4$, its Dirac adjoint is $\\bar z=z^\\dagger\\beta$. The Clifford contraction is $\\Gamma(p):=\\gamma^0p^0-\\sum_{j=1}^3\\gamma^jp_j$; this contraction uses the effective matrix $-\\eta$ in the relation $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Here\n$\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ is the Schwartz space of smooth complex functions\nwhose coordinate-weighted derivatives are all bounded, as defined in\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]].\nFix an oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski space and an inertial coordinate\nchart as in [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use coordinates\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ and metric $\\eta=\\mathrm{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, matching the\n$(-+++)$ convention of the SR suppliers. Some QFT sources use its negative\n$(+---)$; the Lorentz group is unchanged, and the phase is fixed explicitly\nbelow.\nCoordinates $x$ have length units, momenta have inverse-length units, and the\nFourier phase below is dimensionless. A parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ has inverse-length\nunits and is only a mathematical shell parameter; it is not identified with a\nphysical rest mass. For $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3$, set\n$$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2},\\qquad\np_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k).$$\nThe future mass shell is\n$$H_\\mu^+=\\{(p^0,\\mathbf p): (p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2=\\mu^2,\\ p^0>0\\}.$$\nWhen $\\mu=0$, its apex is omitted; thus $\\mathbf k\\ne0$ in the massless\nparameterization. The scalar shell pairing is\n$$p\\circ x:=p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\nDefine the measure and one-particle Hilbert space by\n$$d\\nu_\\mu(p_\\mu(\\mathbf k))=\n\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)},\\qquad\nH_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu;\\mathbb C),$$\nwhere the omitted massless apex has measure zero. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb\nR^4)$ define its pointwise shell Fourier restriction by\n$$K_\\mu f(p)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}f(x)e^{ip\\circ x}\\,d^4x.$$\nThe Fourier convention is exactly the displayed integral, with no prefactor.\nIf $\\widehat f(\\xi^0,\\boldsymbol\\xi)=\n\\int f(x)e^{-2\\pi i(x^0\\xi^0+\\mathbf x\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\xi)}\\,d^4x$ denotes\nthe standard transform in\n[[cor-fourier-transform-is-a-topological-automorphism-of-schwartz-space]],\nthen\n$$\\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p):=\\int f(x)e^{i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)}\\,d^4x\n=\\widehat f\\!\\left(-\\frac{p^0}{2\\pi},\\frac{\\mathbf p}{2\\pi}\\right).$$\nIts inverse is\n$$f(x)=(2\\pi)^{-4}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\n\\mathcal F_+f(p^0,\\mathbf p)e^{-i(p^0x^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf x)}\n\\,dp^0\\,d^3\\mathbf p.$$\nThus this convention is a time reflection and rescaling of the cited\nSchwartz automorphism and has the stated reciprocal normalization.\nThe proper orthochronous Lorentz group consists of linear maps preserving\n$\\eta$, orientation and the chosen future cone. A Poincare map is\n$x\\mapsto a+\\Lambda x$ with $a\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and $\\Lambda$ in that group.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $K(z;\\zeta)=K_0(\\zeta)+zV(\\zeta)$ and $S(z;\\zeta)$ be the finite-dimensional formal matrices of [[def-qft-qed-formal-ring]], with $d\\ge1$, $K_0(\\zeta)$ invertible, and both coefficient matrices covariant under the vertex phase angles of [[def-qft-qed-finite-graph-gauge-data]]. Then $S$ is a two-sided inverse of $K$ in $M_d(\\mathbb C\\llbracket z\\rrbracket)$, and\n$$\nS(z;\\zeta^\\theta)=D_\\theta S(z;\\zeta)D_\\theta^{-1}\n$$\ncoefficientwise. For a differentiable phase path $D_s=e^{is\\Theta}$, where the real vertex-angle path is $s\\theta$, the variations satisfy\n$$\n\\delta K=i[\\Theta,K],\\qquad \\delta S=i[\\Theta,S]=-S(\\delta K)S .\n$$\nFor ordinary finite matrices $A,B\\in GL_d(\\mathbb C)$, the corresponding resolvent identity is $B^{-1}(B-A)A^{-1}=A^{-1}-B^{-1}$. These are formal or finite algebraic statements; they assert no convergence of a perturbation series, continuum limit, or loop Ward identity.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-qed-tree-ward-step-1-1",
          "claim": "From the displayed Pauli matrices in [F1], direct multiplication gives $\\sigma_j^\\dagger=\\sigma_j$, $\\sigma_j^2=I_2$, and $\\sigma_i\\sigma_j+\\sigma_j\\sigma_i=0$ for $i\\ne j$. The block formulas then give $\\beta^2=I_4$, $\\beta^\\dagger=\\beta$, $\\gamma^{0\\dagger}=\\gamma^0$, $\\gamma^{j\\dagger}=-\\gamma^j$, and $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=2h^{ab}I_4$. Consequently $\\Gamma(p)^2=h(p,p)I_4$ and $\\beta\\Gamma(p)^\\dagger\\beta=\\Gamma(p)$ for every real $p$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-qed-tree-ward-step-1-2",
          "claim": "Multiplying $\\Gamma(p)-\\mu I_4$ and $\\Gamma(p)+\\mu I_4$ in either order gives $(h(p,p)-\\mu^2)I_4$ by [step 1.1]. Thus $K(p)$ is invertible off shell and its inverse is exactly $S_0(p)$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-qed-tree-ward-step-1-3",
          "claim": "Since $\\Gamma(p+k)-\\Gamma(p)=\\Gamma(k)$, the resolvent identity [F3] with $A=K(p)$, $B=K(p+k)$ gives $S_0(p+k)\\Gamma(k)S_0(p)=S_0(p)-S_0(p+k)$.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-qed-tree-ward-step-1-4",
          "claim": "For off-shell $p_0,\\ldots,p_N$, put $k_j=p_{j+1}-p_j$. Applying [step 1.3] at each adjacent pair yields $S_0(p_{j+1})\\Gamma(k_j)S_0(p_j)=S_0(p_j)-S_0(p_{j+1})$; summing $j=0,\\ldots,N-1$ cancels each interior term and leaves $S_0(p_0)-S_0(p_N)$.",
          "step": "1.4",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-qed-tree-ward-step-2-1",
          "claim": "If $K(p)u(p)=0$, then taking the adjoint and using $\\beta K(p)^\\dagger\\beta=K(p)$ from [step 1.1] gives $\\bar u(p)K(p)=0$. The same calculation applied to $(\\Gamma(q)+\\mu)v(q)=0$ gives $\\bar v(q)(\\Gamma(q)+\\mu)=0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-qed-tree-ward-step-2-2",
          "claim": "For $k=p-p'$, $\\Gamma(k)=\\Gamma(p)-\\Gamma(p')$; the right equation for $u(p)$ and left equation for $\\bar u(p')$ from [step 2.1] therefore give $\\bar u(p')\\Gamma(k)u(p)=\\mu\\bar u(p')u(p)-\\mu\\bar u(p')u(p)=0$. For $k=p+q$, $\\Gamma(k)=\\Gamma(p)+\\Gamma(q)$; the right $u$ equation and left $v$ equation give $\\bar v(q)\\Gamma(k)u(p)=\\mu\\bar v(q)u(p)-\\mu\\bar v(q)u(p)=0$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
