          "status": "current batch-45 draft supplier; full finite/formal proof inspected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $K(z;\\zeta)=K_0(\\zeta)+zV(\\zeta)$ and $S(z;\\zeta)$ be the finite-dimensional formal matrices of [[def-qft-qed-formal-ring]], with $d\\ge1$, $K_0(\\zeta)$ invertible, and both coefficient matrices covariant under the vertex phase angles of [[def-qft-qed-finite-graph-gauge-data]]. Then $S$ is a two-sided inverse of $K$ in $M_d(\\mathbb C\\llbracket z\\rrbracket)$, and\n$$\nS(z;\\zeta^\\theta)=D_\\theta S(z;\\zeta)D_\\theta^{-1}\n$$\ncoefficientwise. For a differentiable phase path $D_s=e^{is\\Theta}$, where the real vertex-angle path is $s\\theta$, the variations satisfy\n$$\n\\delta K=i[\\Theta,K],\\qquad \\delta S=i[\\Theta,S]=-S(\\delta K)S .\n$$\nFor ordinary finite matrices $A,B\\in GL_d(\\mathbb C)$, the corresponding resolvent identity is $B^{-1}(B-A)A^{-1}=A^{-1}-B^{-1}$. These are formal or finite algebraic statements; they assert no convergence of a perturbation series, continuum limit, or loop Ward identity.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F3] [[lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward]]: finite invertible matrices obey the resolvent identity $B^{-1}(B-A)A^{-1}=A^{-1}-B^{-1}$, and the formal inverse variation is coefficientwise.\nConsumer uses: 1.3.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current assigned draft; proof is complete here, but readiness is escalated through its QFT-MG01 input.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in step 1.3"
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "authoring-self-check",
        "reviewer": "beta-batch-45-authoring",
        "notes": "Checked the exact conditional supplier interfaces against the cited uses. This record is not independent proof approval."
      }
    },
    "lem-qft-qed-grassmann-determinant": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-qed-finite-grassmann-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Order the generators as $\\bar\\psi_1,\\ldots,\\bar\\psi_d,\\psi_1,\\ldots,\\psi_d$. The corresponding ordered monomials form the $R$-basis of $\\Lambda_d(R)$. Let\n$$\nM_d:=\\bar\\psi_1\\cdots\\bar\\psi_d\\psi_1\\cdots\\psi_d\n$$\nand let $[M_d]P\\in R$ denote the coefficient of $M_d$ in $P\\in\\Lambda_d(R)$. Define the finite Grassmann integral by the $R$-linear functional\n$$\n\\mathcal I_d(P):=(-1)^{d(d+1)/2}[M_d]P .\n$$\nFor $K\\in M_d(R)$, put $B_K:=\\sum_{i,j=1}^d\\bar\\psi_iK_{ij}\\psi_j$. Since every monomial of degree greater than $2d$ vanishes, its exponential is the finite sum\n$$\n\\exp(-B_K):=\\sum_{n=0}^{d}\\frac{(-B_K)^n}{n!}.\n$$\nThe sign factor in $\\mathcal I_d$ is the chosen normalization. Its Gaussian determinant identity, including the value at $K=I_d$, is proved separately.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qft-qed-formal-ring",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the finite graph phase data of [[def-qft-qed-finite-graph-gauge-data]] and the planned QED $U(1)$, Clifford, and natural-unit conventions of [[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]]. Let $K_0(\\zeta)\\in GL_d(\\mathbb C)$ be an invertible base matrix and let $V(\\zeta)\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ be its finite link-hopping matrix. The admissible covariant families satisfy\n$$\nK_0(\\zeta^\\alpha)=D_\\alpha K_0(\\zeta)D_\\alpha^{-1},\\qquad\nV(\\zeta^\\alpha)=D_\\alpha V(\\zeta)D_\\alpha^{-1}.\n$$\nThe scalar base $K_0=mI_d$, $m\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$, is an explicit admissible case. Define\n$$\nK(z;\\zeta):=K_0(\\zeta)+zV(\\zeta),\\qquad\nS(z;\\zeta):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(-zK_0(\\zeta)^{-1}V(\\zeta))^nK_0(\\zeta)^{-1}.\n$$\nThe coefficient of each power $z^n$ in $S$ is one finite matrix. The item defines this formal candidate; the following lemma proves its two-sided inverse and covariance properties. Define $\\det A$ for a finite matrix $A$ over $\\mathbb C\\llbracket z\\rrbracket$ by the Leibniz formula $\\det A=\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_d}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)\\prod_{j=1}^d A_{j,\\sigma(j)}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-qed-grassmann-determinant-step-1-1",
          "claim": "Put $B_K=\\sum_{i,j}\\bar\\psi_iK_{ij}\\psi_j$. Every factor has Grassmann degree two, so a product of more than $d$ such factors has degree greater than $2d$ and vanishes. Hence the exponential is the finite sum $\\sum_{n=0}^d(-B_K)^n/n!$, and only its degree-$2d$ term contributes to $[M_d]$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-qed-grassmann-determinant-step-1-2",
          "claim": "In $B_K^d/d!$, any term with a repeated row or column index vanishes. The surviving terms are indexed by permutations $\\sigma\\in S_d$. Reordering $\\bar\\psi_1\\psi_{\\sigma(1)}\\cdots\\bar\\psi_d\\psi_{\\sigma(d)}$ into $M_d$ contributes $(-1)^{d(d-1)/2}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)$; the exponential contributes $(-1)^d$. Therefore\n$\n[M_d]\\exp(-B_K)=(-1)^{d(d+1)/2}\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_d}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)\\prod_{i=1}^dK_{i,\\sigma(i)}\n=(-1)^{d(d+1)/2}\\det K .\n$\nApplying the normalization in [F1] proves the Gaussian identity.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-qft-qed-grassmann-determinant-step-2-1",
          "claim": "For diagonal $D$, $K'_{ij}=u_iK_{ij}u_j^{-1}$. In each Leibniz term, $\\prod_i u_i\\prod_i u_{\\sigma(i)}^{-1}=1$, since $\\sigma$ permutes the indices; thus $\\det K'=\\det K$. The Gaussian identity from [step 1.2] then gives the same equality for the finite Grassmann integral.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "A1 requires d≥1, so the empty-generator case is excluded; step 1.1 uses top degree 2d."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "For K=0 and d≥1 the exponential is 1 and its top coefficient and determinant vanish by step 1.2."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "For d=1, I₁(1−barψ₁K₁₁ψ₁)=K₁₁, the one-by-one determinant in step 1.2."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "No invertibility is assumed; singular K is covered by the permutation formula in steps 1.2 and 2.1."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The lem-qft-qed-grassmann-determinant claim makes no endpoints boundary assertion."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The lem-qft-qed-grassmann-determinant claim makes no nonempty-choice boundary assertion."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The lem-qft-qed-grassmann-determinant claim makes no iff-forward boundary assertion."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The lem-qft-qed-grassmann-determinant claim makes no iff-reverse boundary assertion."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "name": "one-generator Gaussian",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2"
          ],
          "expected": "For d=1, exp(−barψKψ)=1−barψKψ and I₁ returns K."
        }
      ],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-qft-qed-finite-grassmann-algebra",
          "carrier": "items/def-qft-qed-finite-grassmann-algebra.md",
          "status": "current batch-45 supplier; exact current interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Order the generators as $\\bar\\psi_1,\\ldots,\\bar\\psi_d,\\psi_1,\\ldots,\\psi_d$. The corresponding ordered monomials form the $R$-basis of $\\Lambda_d(R)$. Let\n$$\nM_d:=\\bar\\psi_1\\cdots\\bar\\psi_d\\psi_1\\cdots\\psi_d\n$$\nand let $[M_d]P\\in R$ denote the coefficient of $M_d$ in $P\\in\\Lambda_d(R)$. Define the finite Grassmann integral by the $R$-linear functional\n$$\n\\mathcal I_d(P):=(-1)^{d(d+1)/2}[M_d]P .\n$$\nFor $K\\in M_d(R)$, put $B_K:=\\sum_{i,j=1}^d\\bar\\psi_iK_{ij}\\psi_j$. Since every monomial of degree greater than $2d$ vanishes, its exponential is the finite sum\n$$\n\\exp(-B_K):=\\sum_{n=0}^{d}\\frac{(-B_K)^n}{n!}.\n$$\nThe sign factor in $\\mathcal I_d$ is the chosen normalization. Its Gaussian determinant identity, including the value at $K=I_d$, is proved separately.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F1] [[def-qft-qed-finite-grassmann-algebra]] defines anticommuting generators, the ordered top monomial $M_d$, the coefficient functional $\\mathcal I_d(P)=(-1)^{d(d+1)/2}[M_d]P$, and the finite exponential.\nConsumer uses: 1.1, 1.2.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current assigned item; use only at the inspected statement and proof status.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 is used in step 1.1; step 1.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-qft-qed-formal-ring",
          "carrier": "items/def-qft-qed-formal-ring.md",
          "status": "current batch-45 supplier; exact current interface inspected",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Use the finite graph phase data of [[def-qft-qed-finite-graph-gauge-data]] and the planned QED $U(1)$, Clifford, and natural-unit conventions of [[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]]. Let $K_0(\\zeta)\\in GL_d(\\mathbb C)$ be an invertible base matrix and let $V(\\zeta)\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ be its finite link-hopping matrix. The admissible covariant families satisfy\n$$\nK_0(\\zeta^\\alpha)=D_\\alpha K_0(\\zeta)D_\\alpha^{-1},\\qquad\nV(\\zeta^\\alpha)=D_\\alpha V(\\zeta)D_\\alpha^{-1}.\n$$\nThe scalar base $K_0=mI_d$, $m\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$, is an explicit admissible case. Define\n$$\nK(z;\\zeta):=K_0(\\zeta)+zV(\\zeta),\\qquad\nS(z;\\zeta):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(-zK_0(\\zeta)^{-1}V(\\zeta))^nK_0(\\zeta)^{-1}.\n$$\nThe coefficient of each power $z^n$ in $S$ is one finite matrix. The item defines this formal candidate; the following lemma proves its two-sided inverse and covariance properties. Define $\\det A$ for a finite matrix $A$ over $\\mathbb C\\llbracket z\\rrbracket$ by the Leibniz formula $\\det A=\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_d}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)\\prod_{j=1}^d A_{j,\\sigma(j)}$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F2] [[def-qft-qed-formal-ring]] defines $\\det K$ by the finite Leibniz sum and the diagonal vertex-phase conjugation convention.\nConsumer uses: 1.2, 2.1.",
